diff --git a/mlst/01_intro.tex b/mlst/01_intro.tex
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..306aa1d
--- /dev/null
+++ b/mlst/01_intro.tex
@@ -0,0 +1,144 @@
+\section{Introduction}
+\label{sec:mlst-intro}
+
+Scientific detectors record a structured response superposed with electronic,
+thermal and environmental fluctuations that are rarely independent and
+identically distributed: they are colored in frequency, heteroskedastic across
+samples, correlated between readout channels, or slowly varying in time.
+Reconstruction must therefore do more than smooth a waveform.  It must avoid
+spending model capacity on directions dominated by detector noise.
+
+Three communities solve closely related problems in different language.
+Signal processing projects a trace onto a known template with inverse-noise
+weighting \citep{turin1960introduction,kay1993fundamentals}; in cryogenic
+calorimetry this is the optimal-filter (OF) amplitude estimator
+\citep{szymkowiak1993signal,fixsen1993pulse,kurinsky2021}.  Statistics
+replaces the fixed template by a learned low-dimensional model --- probabilistic
+PCA under isotropic noise \citep{tipping1999probabilistic}, factor analysis
+under diagonal noise \citep{rubin1982algorithms}, weighted or
+expectation--maximization PCA (EMPCA) under nonuniform weights
+\citep{bailey2012principal,yu2020optimal}.  Machine learning writes the same
+projection as an encoder, a latent code and a decoder; under Euclidean
+mean-squared error a tied linear autoencoder recovers the PCA subspace
+\citep{baldi1989neural,bourlard1988auto,plaut2018principal}.
+
+The common object is the observation-space metric.  For $x=s+n$ with
+$n\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\Sigma)$, the Gaussian negative log-likelihood of a
+candidate reconstruction $\hat s$ contains $(x-\hat s)^{\dagger}\Sigma^{-1}(x-\hat s)$,
+so $\Sigma^{-1}$ decides which residual directions are costly.  Isotropic MSE
+is not a neutral default: it is the Gaussian likelihood only when
+$\Sigma=\sigma^{2}I$, and under colored noise it ranks residuals in a
+different geometry.  A method can reduce raw MSE while worsening the
+covariance-weighted residual that follows from the assumed detector model.
+That is a likelihood misspecification, not a change of units.
+
+\paragraph{The question.}
+What determines which representation a detector model learns, and when does
+covariance weighting --- or a structural restriction of the learned class ---
+improve recovery of a physical signal?  For a calibrated readout the theory
+exposes three determinants: the \emph{metric} the measurement fixes, the
+\emph{class} the architecture fixes, and the \emph{support} the training data
+excite.  This paper establishes the first two, states the third as an exact
+population identity, and then asks what each does at finite sample, where the
+covariance is estimated and the data are limited.  The answers are mostly
+negative in the places where the finite-sample question bites, and the paper
+reports them that way.
+
+\paragraph{Contributions.}
+\begin{enumerate}
+\item[(i)] \emph{One projection, four classes.}  OF, covariance-weighted PCA,
+the tied noise-aware linear autoencoder and its Kronecker-separable
+restriction are one $\Sigma^{-1}$-orthogonal projection whose classes differ
+only in the admissible set, with the tied encoder as an adjoint condition and
+untying stated as a change of parameterization rather than of optimum
+(Section~\ref{sec:mlst-theory}).
+\item[(ii)] \emph{Which risk the residual predicts.}  The weighted residual
+predicts the population whitened clean-signal error of a fixed linear map
+exactly, up to a trace correction; it need not predict Euclidean clean-signal
+error, and a two-dimensional example separates them
+(Section~\ref{subsec:mlst-risks}).
+\item[(iii)] \emph{Covariance error acts locally.}  To first order the learned
+subspace moves by the retained--discarded coupling block of the relative
+covariance error divided by the pairwise eigenvalue gaps
+(Proposition~\ref{prop:mlst-local-displacement}).  A diagonal error in the
+fitted eigenbasis is exactly harmless until it reorders the spectrum; a
+near-singular estimate is a separate regime.  In a controlled study,
+perturbations of equal norm differed by two orders of magnitude in effect,
+as the local statement predicts and the global norm bound cannot
+(Section~\ref{subsec:mlst-cov-anatomy}).
+\item[(iv)] \emph{A structural prior trades approximation for estimation.}
+The finite-sample contrast between the separable and the unrestricted class
+decomposes exactly into a population approximation cost, for which a
+computable lower bound is given, and two estimation excesses
+(Proposition~\ref{prop:mlst-risk-decomposition}).  On fresh seeds the
+restricted learner won at $N=800$ and lost at $N=1600$: both regimes were
+observed and the crossover is bracketed, not located
+(Section~\ref{subsec:mlst-structural-prior}).
+\item[(v)] \emph{Excited is not detectable.}  Scaling the excitation of one
+physical latent in the training draw did not set its recovery.  All five
+registered predictions of that study failed; an exploratory reading in terms
+of the induced population spectrum and the direction's eigenvector loadings is
+recorded for an independent test (Section~\ref{subsec:mlst-excitation}).
+\item[(vi)] \emph{An evidence programme with named boundaries.}  For each
+public dataset we report which of three questions --- reconstruction in the
+assumed geometry, calibration of the assumed covariance, agreement with
+recorded truth in a named metric --- it can and cannot answer, including the
+failures (Table~\ref{tab:mlst-instruments}).
+\end{enumerate}
+
+\paragraph{What is recalled and what is added.}
+The identities organizing (i) are largely classical: that the generalized
+least-squares amplitude is the optimal filter, that covariance-weighted
+low-rank approximation reduces to an SVD of whitened data
+\citep{gabriel1979lower,allen2014gls}, that Euclidean linear autoencoders
+recover PCA \citep{baldi1989neural}, and that alternating per-axis projections
+are multilinear PCA \citep{lu2008mpca}.  Statement (ii) specializes classical
+unbiased-risk accounting \citep{mallows1973cp,stein1981estimation} to a fixed
+linear map in whitened coordinates; (iii) is first-order invariant-subspace
+perturbation theory \citep{stewart1990matrix} applied to the operator the
+estimator actually diagonalizes; (iv) is the ordinary
+approximation--estimation decomposition of a constrained estimator with a
+Ky~Fan bound.  What is added is their statement in one geometry, and what the
+controlled programme found when it tested them.  No statement here is claimed
+as an independently substantiated novelty; \sup{S1} marks each as established,
+specialized, a corollary, a synthesis, or derived here.
+
+\paragraph{Evidence arc.}
+Controlled simulations first test consequences that are known under the
+data-generating model.  Public CRESST-II/III pulse-shape data
+\citep{angloher2025cresstpulshape} then give a positive but narrow
+real-detector test of within-channel reconstruction.  Public H1/L1 data around
+GW150914 \citep{gwoscgw150914} give a negative calibration stress test.
+Neither release records the signal that was present, so both compare residuals
+rather than distance to truth; public TIDMAD data from the ABRACADABRA axion
+search \citep{fry2024tidmad} supply the missing ground truth through an
+injected reference channel, and deliver two positive and two negative results.
+This order separates reconstruction performance from detection sensitivity,
+and implementation identity from statistical calibration.
+
+\ifgatedevidence
+On planted physics we then test whether the wrong metric learns the wrong
+physical structure (Section~\ref{subsec:separable-evidence}).
+\else
+One block of controlled planted-physics evidence for the separable class ---
+whether the wrong metric learns the wrong physical structure, and how much of
+the excited span a finite sample recovers --- is complete and is held from this
+version until the double-blind review of its companion workshop submission
+concludes on 29~September 2026.  Nothing else is held; the September programme
+on the same planted family is reported in full
+(Section~\ref{sec:mlst-controlled}).
+\fi
+
+\paragraph{Scope.}
+The scope is linear and Gaussian.  The exact statements do not extend to
+nonlinear autoencoders, non-Gaussian transients, or a covariance model that is
+wrong for the evaluation data.  CRESST is used only for within-channel
+reconstruction of public held-out pulses, not cross-channel transfer or
+detector sensitivity.  GWOSC is used to test whether two specific PSD models
+calibrate a matched statistic on one public interval, not to estimate GW150914
+significance.  TIDMAD is used at named operating points on named validation
+files; it is not a survey of the release's parameter space.  Detection
+sensitivity is not claimed anywhere.  Full derivations, proofs, implementation
+details, verification grids and per-file diagnostics are in the technical
+supplement, and every number traces to a checksummed record
+(Section~\ref{sec:mlst-availability}).
