+\section{Observation model and target risks}
+\label{sec:mlst-model}
+
+\subsection{Model, whitening and the common objective}
+\label{subsec:mlst-objective}
+
+Write the measurement as
+\begin{equation}
+    x_i = s_i + n_i,\qquad
+    n_i\sim\mathcal{N}_{\mathbb{C}}(0,\Sigma),\qquad
+    \Sigma\succ0,\qquad i=1,\dots,N,
+    \label{eq:mlst-generative}
+\end{equation}
+with $x_i\in\mathbb{C}^{d}$ a single trace (or a vectorized channel--time
+block) and $s_i$ the structured response.  The complex circular convention is
+used throughout; the real case is identical with $\dagger\to T$.  Let
+$\Sigma=\Sigma^{1/2}\Sigma^{\dagger/2}$ and define the whitening map
+$y=\Sigma^{-1/2}x$, under which the noise becomes isotropic,
+$\Sigma^{-1/2}n\sim\mathcal{N}_{\mathbb{C}}(0,I)$.
+
+Up to constants the Gaussian negative log-likelihood of a reconstruction
+$\hat s$ is
+\begin{equation}
+    \chi^{2}(\hat s)=(x-\hat s)^{\dagger}\Sigma^{-1}(x-\hat s)
+    =\bigl\|\Sigma^{-1/2}(x-\hat s)\bigr\|_{2}^{2},
+    \label{eq:mlst-chi2}
+\end{equation}
+so every method in this paper solves one problem --- minimize
+\eqref{eq:mlst-chi2} --- over a different admissible set $\mathcal{F}$ for
+$\hat s$.  Fixing $\hat s\in\operatorname{span}\{p\}$ for a known template $p$
+gives the optimal filter; letting $\hat s$ range over a learned
+$k$-dimensional subspace gives covariance-weighted (EMPCA) reconstruction;
+parameterizing that subspace by an encoder and decoder gives the tied
+noise-aware linear autoencoder; restricting it to a Kronecker product of a
+channel and a time subspace gives the separable class.  The measurement fixes
+the metric, the architecture fixes the class.
+
+By contrast the isotropic loss $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{MSE}}=N^{-1}\sum_i\|x_i-\hat x_i\|_2^{2}$
+coincides with \eqref{eq:mlst-chi2} up to scale exactly when
+$\Sigma=\sigma^{2}I$.  The two losses define different inner products, hence
+different notions of which waveform directions matter: isotropic MSE weights
+every bin equally and is driven by raw variance, which under colored noise is
+typically dominated by noise-rich, low-information directions, while the
+$\Sigma^{-1}$-weighted loss is driven by signal-to-noise structure.  The same
+class, rank, data and optimizer can therefore converge to substantially
+different subspaces.  We call the resulting failure mode \emph{metric
+reversal}: a method can achieve lower raw MSE together with a worse weighted
+residual --- equivalently a worse held-out Gaussian likelihood --- while
+learning a subspace nearly orthogonal, in the whitened metric, to the one
+efficient estimation requires.
+
+\subsection{Which risk the weighted residual predicts}
+\label{subsec:mlst-risks}
+
+The likelihood fixes a residual geometry.  It does not by itself say which
+scientific error a reconstruction chosen in that geometry makes small, so
+every claim below names its target.  For a reconstruction $\hat x=\hat x(x)$
+of $x=s+n$ four risks are in play: the observation residual
+$\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{obs}}=\mathbb{E}\,(x-\hat x)^{T}\Sigma^{-1}(x-\hat x)$;
+the whitened clean-signal risk
+$\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{clean},\Sigma}=\mathbb{E}\,(s-\hat x)^{T}\Sigma^{-1}(s-\hat x)$;
+the Euclidean clean-signal risk
+$\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{clean},I}=\mathbb{E}\,\|s-\hat x\|_{2}^{2}$; and the
+physical-parameter risk
+$\mathcal{R}_{\vartheta}=\mathbb{E}\,\|\hat\vartheta-\vartheta\|^{2}$.
+
+\begin{proposition}[Fixed affine-estimator risk identity]
+\label{prop:mlst-risk-identity}
+Work in real whitened coordinates, $y=\Sigma^{-1/2}x=t+w$.  Let $\hat y=By+b$
+be an affine reconstruction with $(B,b)$ fixed --- fitted on other data, or
+population quantities --- and let the evaluation noise satisfy, conditionally
+on $(B,b)$, $\mathbb{E}[w]=0$, $\mathbb{E}[ww^{T}]=I$ and
+$\mathbb{E}[tw^{T}]=0$.  Then
+\begin{equation}
+    \mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{clean},\Sigma}
+    =\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{obs}}+2\operatorname{tr}(B)-d .
+    \label{eq:mlst-risk-correction}
+\end{equation}
+Every idempotent $B$ of rank $k$ --- orthogonal or oblique --- has
+$\operatorname{tr}B=k$ and correction $2k-d$; a shrinkage estimator
+$B=\sum_j\beta_ju_ju_j^{T}$ has correction $2\sum_j\beta_j-d$.
+\end{proposition}
+
+The proof is two lines of unbiased-risk accounting
+\citep{mallows1973cp,stein1981estimation} and is in \sup{S2}.  Four
+consequences bound what the rest of the paper may claim.
+\emph{(a) Population and conditional.}  The identity is a population
+statement conditional on the fitted $(B,b)$; its moment conditions must hold
+for the evaluation noise given the training data.  A chronological split makes
+them plausible but does not establish them under dependent noise, and a wrong
+$\Sigma$ violates $\mathbb{E}[ww^{T}]=I$ and biases the correction.
+\emph{(b) Ordering.}  Within any set of fixed estimators of equal trace ---
+in particular all rank-$k$ idempotent maps, so that a raw-PCA projector
+expressed in true whitened coordinates is covered --- ordering by the
+observation residual is ordering by the whitened clean-signal risk.  That is
+the precise sense in which the covariance-weighted subspace is the better
+reconstruction.  On a finite evaluation sample the two orderings can differ,
+so every comparison below carries its own interval.
+\emph{(c) Roles.}  Training minimizes a sample objective; a held-out residual
+estimates the population risk of the fitted map; validation selects among
+fitted maps; a final test compares the selected maps once.  The identity
+connects the second of these to the clean-signal risk and says nothing about
+the first.
+\emph{(d) Silent about other targets.}  Maps of different trace need the
+correction; a nonlinear or data-dependent map is outside the identity; and
+neither the Euclidean clean-signal risk nor a physical-parameter risk is
+implied.  Training on a clean target with isotropic MSE is therefore not an
+assertion that the noise is white --- it is the choice of
+$\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{clean},I}$ as the prediction risk, a legitimate target
+that the weighted residual need not predict.
+
+\begin{example}[The two clean-signal risks can order oppositely]
+\label{ex:mlst-risk-counterexample}
+Let $d=2$, $k=1$, with independent zero-mean Gaussian signal and noise,
+$\mathbb{E}[ss^{T}]=\operatorname{diag}(100,30)$ and
+$\Sigma=\operatorname{diag}(4,1)$.  Raw PCA retains $e_1$ (observed variances
+$104,31$); weighted PCA retains $e_2$ (whitened observed variances $26,31$).
+With the exact covariance, infinite data and exact optimization,
+$\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{obs}}$ and $\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{clean},\Sigma}$ are
+$31$ against $26$ --- both prefer weighted PCA, as
+Proposition~\ref{prop:mlst-risk-identity} requires with $2k-d=0$ --- while
+$\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{clean},I}$ is $34$ against $101$ and prefers raw PCA,
+because the Euclidean clean error of the projector onto $e_j$ is the signal
+variance it discards plus the noise variance it keeps.  The example separates
+the objective from another legitimate risk: a disagreement between the two
+orderings is not by itself evidence for the likelihood-aligned one.
+\end{example}
+
+\subsection{Three testable predictions}
+\label{subsec:mlst-predictions}
+
+Holding class, rank, data, preprocessing and split fixed and changing only the
+loss geometry gives three predictions, which organize the evidence of
+Sections~\ref{sec:mlst-controlled}--\ref{sec:mlst-instruments}:
+\textbf{(P1)} under white noise, isotropic PCA and EMPCA recover the same
+subspace and agree on all metrics --- an internal control, since if the two
+coincide at $\Sigma\propto I$ and separate under colored noise, the effect is
+attributable to loss geometry rather than to rank, optimization or
+preprocessing;
+\textbf{(P2)} under colored noise the objectives diverge, each winning in the
+metric it optimizes;
+\textbf{(P3)} where a clean signal or injected reference is recorded, the
+reconstruction preferred by the weighted residual also has the lower
+clean-signal error in a named metric.  For the whitened clean-signal risk and
+fixed maps of equal trace, (P3) is the population content of
+Proposition~\ref{prop:mlst-risk-identity}; for the Euclidean risk it is not
+implied (Example~\ref{ex:mlst-risk-counterexample}); for a physical parameter
+scored against a reference --- what the TIDMAD stage measures --- it is an
+empirical hypothesis of this paper.  (P3) is what distinguishes the claim from
+a tautology, because (P2) alone says only that each objective wins in its own
+metric.  (P1) and (P2) are testable on any release; (P3) needs a dataset that
+records the injected signal.
+
+A gain in the weighted residual is not by itself a gain in energy resolution
+or detector sensitivity, which additionally require a frozen
+signal-injection and background protocol; none is claimed here.
+
+\subsection{Pulling the metric back onto a representation}
+\label{subsec:mlst-pullback}
+
+Two further consequences of the same metric are needed to say what the
+controlled evidence measures.  For a forward model $x=g(\vartheta)+n$ with
+physical latents $\vartheta\in\mathbb{R}^{m}$ and Jacobian $J_g$, the Fisher
+information of $\vartheta$ is $I(\vartheta)=J_g^{T}\Sigma^{-1}J_g$: the
+likelihood \emph{pulls the $\Sigma^{-1}$ inner product back} onto latent
+space, and a latent displacement's length is the distance it moves the
+observation in units of what the noise can resolve.  The same construction
+applies to any representation $h$ whose prediction is compared with $x$ under
+$\Sigma$, giving $J_h^{T}\Sigma^{-1}J_h$, degenerate on $\ker J_h$.  A
+Euclidean latent distance is therefore the wrong statistic in the same sense
+that isotropic MSE is --- with the same qualification, that the two coincide
+wherever the pulled-back form is a multiple of the identity, as in whitened
+orthonormal coordinates.  Where $I(\vartheta)$ is singular the latent
+coordinates are not locally identifiable to first order; that is a statement
+about rate, not about injectivity of $g$.
+
+Second, if the training distribution varies only the latent coordinates
+$S$, write $T_S=\operatorname{range}(\Sigma^{-1/2}A_{\cdot S})$ for the
+excited span in whitened coordinates and $R_t$ for the second moment of the
+whitened signal displacement.  The population rank-$k$ objective depends on
+the projector only through $\operatorname{tr}(\Pi R_t)$, so it is
+\emph{exactly} invariant under every rotation fixing $T_S$ pointwise.  With
+$r=\operatorname{rank}R_t$: if $k\ge r$ the minimizers are exactly the
+projectors containing $\operatorname{range}R_t$, and the remaining $k-r$
+dimensions are not determined by the population objective; if $k<r$ they are
+the top-$k$ eigenspaces of $R_t$, contained in $T_S$.  Unexcited directions
+are therefore invisible to the population objective, and a finite sample
+recovers the excited span only above its own sampling-noise floor.  The full
+statement and proof are in \sup{S3}.
+
+The three determinants are now explicit: the measurement fixes the metric
+$\Sigma^{-1}$, the architecture fixes the class $\mathcal{F}$, and the
+training data fix the excited support $S$.  Sections~\ref{sec:mlst-theory}
+and~\ref{sec:mlst-estimators} establish the first two;
+Section~\ref{sec:mlst-controlled} asks what each does at finite sample.
