+\section{Recovery theory: one projection, four classes}
+\label{sec:mlst-theory}
+
+\subsection{The hierarchy}
+\label{subsec:mlst-hierarchy}
+
+Every estimator in this paper minimizes \eqref{eq:mlst-chi2} over a different
+admissible set.  Table~\ref{tab:mlst-hierarchy} is the whole hierarchy;
+Figure~\ref{fig:mlst-hierarchy} draws it as one projection whose faces are
+transposes.  The classes are nested, $\text{OF}\subset\text{NFPA}\subset\text{EMPCA}$,
+with the tied linear autoencoder a \emph{parameterization} of the EMPCA class
+rather than a fifth class.
+
+\begin{table}[!htbp]
+\centering
+\small
+\caption{One $\Sigma^{-1}$-orthogonal projection, four admissible classes.
+$C$ channels, $T$ time samples, $d=CT$.  ``Degenerates to'' names the exact
+finite-sample coincidence, not an asymptotic one.}
+\label{tab:mlst-hierarchy}
+\begin{tabular}{@{}llll@{}}
+\toprule
+class & admissible $\hat s$ & free parameters & degenerates to \\
+\midrule
+optimal filter & $\alpha p$, $p$ known & $\alpha\in\mathbb{C}$ & --- \\
+EMPCA (rank $k$) & $\hat s\in\mathcal{S}$, $\dim\mathcal{S}=k$ & Grassmannian $\mathrm{Gr}(k,d)$ & OF at $k=1$, $p\in\mathcal{S}$ \\
+tied linear AE & as EMPCA, $P^{\dagger}\Sigma^{-1}P=I_k$ & $P\in\mathbb{C}^{d\times k}$ & EMPCA exactly (Thm.~\ref{thm:mlst-bridge}) \\
+NFPA (separable) & $\mathcal{S}=\mathcal{S}_c\otimes\mathcal{S}_t$ & $\mathrm{Gr}(k_c,C)\times\mathrm{Gr}(k_t,T)$ & EMPCA iff optimum separable; OF at $(1,1)$ \\
+\bottomrule
+\end{tabular}
+\end{table}
+
+\begin{figure}[!tp]
+    \centering
+    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth,height=0.60\textheight,keepaspectratio]{evidence/figures/likelihood_geometry_hierarchy.pdf}
+    \caption{The estimators as one $\Sigma^{-1}$-orthogonal projection,
+    differing only in the admissible column space of $P$.  Rank-1 optimal
+    filtering fixes that column space to a known template; EMPCA learns a
+    $k$-dimensional subspace over the full Grassmannian; the tied linear
+    autoencoder writes the same subspace as an encoder and a decoder related
+    by $P^{\dagger}\Sigma^{-1}P=I_k$, so the two faces are transposes.  Block
+    widths and heights encode matrix shapes; cell counts illustrate axis
+    structure and are not literal dimensions.}
+    \label{fig:mlst-hierarchy}
+\end{figure}
+
+\subsection{The bridge to representation learning}
+\label{subsec:mlst-bridge}
+
+\begin{theorem}[Bridge Theorem: noise-aware linear AE $\equiv$ EMPCA]
+\label{thm:mlst-bridge}
+Let $x_1,\dots,x_N\in\mathbb{C}^{d}$ be any observations, $\Sigma\succ0$ any
+Hermitian weight, $1\le k\le d$.  Consider the tied noise-aware loss
+\begin{equation}
+    \mathcal{L}(P)=\frac1N\sum_{i=1}^{N}
+    (x_i-PP^{\dagger}\Sigma^{-1}x_i)^{\dagger}\Sigma^{-1}(x_i-PP^{\dagger}\Sigma^{-1}x_i),
+    \qquad P^{\dagger}\Sigma^{-1}P=I_k,
+    \label{eq:mlst-bridge-loss}
+\end{equation}
+and let $\lambda_1\ge\cdots\ge\lambda_d\ge0$ be the eigenvalues of the whitened
+second moment $\Sigma^{-1/2}\hat R_x\Sigma^{-1/2}$.  Then $P^{*}$ minimizes
+$\mathcal{L}$ if and only if $\Sigma^{-1/2}P^{*}$ spans a top-$k$ invariant
+subspace of that operator --- equivalently, $P^{*}$ spans the top-$k$
+generalized eigenspace of $\hat R_x\Sigma^{-1}v=\lambda v$ --- and
+$\mathcal{L}^{*}=\operatorname{tr}(\Sigma^{-1}\hat R_x)-\sum_{j\le k}\lambda_j$.
+If $\lambda_k>\lambda_{k+1}$ the subspace is unique and equals the rank-$k$
+EMPCA range, up to a unitary rotation within it.  If $\lambda_k=\lambda_{k+1}$
+the minimizers form a family; in particular when $\operatorname{rank}\hat R_x<k$
+every direction of $\Sigma\ker\hat R_x$ --- the null space of
+$\hat R_x\Sigma^{-1}$, \emph{not} of $\hat R_x$ --- is an equally good
+completion.
+\end{theorem}
+
+Whitening turns the constraint into $W^{\dagger}W=I_k$ and the loss into
+$N^{-1}\|\tilde X(I-WW^{\dagger})\|_F^{2}$, so Rayleigh--Ritz gives the result;
+the proof is in \sup{S4}.
+
+\begin{remark}[What the theorem uses, and what it does not]
+\label{rem:mlst-bridge-scope}
+The bridge is a \emph{deterministic identity} between two optimization
+problems: it holds for any data, any $\Sigma\succ0$ and any $N\ge1$, and its
+proof uses no noise model.  The measurement model enters only in motivating
+the weight.  Three distinctions matter for reading the evidence.  The theorem
+characterizes the \emph{global} minimizers, whereas the alternating algorithm
+reaches a stationary point and every $k$-dimensional invariant subspace is
+stationary, so agreement with the global optimum is a property of the
+initialization and the spectrum.  The minimizer is unique only up to the
+latent gauge $P^{*}\mapsto P^{*}Q$, $Q\in\mathcal{U}(k)$; physical quantities
+depend on the reconstruction $P^{*}P^{*\dagger}\Sigma^{-1}x$ and are
+gauge-invariant.  And \emph{untying} the encoder changes neither the optimal
+reconstruction nor the class --- only the parameterization and the landscape
+(\sup{S5}).
+\end{remark}
+
+\subsection{The metric is architecture-neutral}
+\label{subsec:mlst-architecture-neutral}
+
+The weighted loss is not one modeling choice among many.  For quadratic
+residual losses $\mathcal{L}_M(r)=r^{\dagger}Mr$ with $M\succ0$, invariance
+under the isometry group of the measured noise --- the maps preserving
+$\Sigma$ --- is \emph{equivalent} to $M\propto\Sigma^{-1}$ (\sup{S6}).  The
+argument acts on the residual vector alone and mentions no architecture, so a
+reconstruction method is the pair $(\Sigma^{-1},\mathcal{F})$ of a residual
+geometry fixed by the likelihood and an admissible class fixed by the
+architecture.  This is what makes ``metric'' and ``class'' separable
+determinants rather than two names for the same choice.
+
+A related invariance is worth stating because it is often assumed rather than
+checked: the projector $PP^{\dagger}\Sigma^{-1}$ and every reconstruction
+derived from it are invariant under the joint rescaling
+$(\Sigma,x)\mapsto(c^{2}\Sigma,cx)$, so nothing in the hierarchy depends on
+the physical units of the readout; only the relative shape of $\Sigma$ enters.
+
+\subsection{Covariance error acts locally}
+\label{subsec:mlst-local}
+
+In practice $\Sigma$ is estimated.  Write the supplied covariance as
+$\hat\Sigma=\Sigma^{1/2}(I+E)\Sigma^{1/2}$ with $E$ Hermitian and
+$\varepsilon=\|E\|_{\mathrm{op}}<1$; $\varepsilon$ is the natural
+\emph{relative} error, invariant under the rescaling above.  A Davis--Kahan
+argument gives the global bound $\sin\Theta\le6\varepsilon\lambda_1/\Delta_\lambda$
+for $\varepsilon\le1/2$ (\sup{S7}).  That bound is valid but, where it applies,
+usually uninformative: on the planted two-axis configuration of
+Section~\ref{subsec:mlst-structural-prior} it is vacuous at any $\varepsilon$
+of practical size, because $\lambda_1/\Delta_\lambda\approx2.7\times10^{3}$ at
+rank six.  The local statement is the informative one.
+
+\begin{proposition}[First-order displacement under covariance error]
+\label{prop:mlst-local-displacement}
+Let $A$ be the whitened operator whose top-$k$ eigenspace the estimator
+returns, with eigenpairs $(\lambda_i,q_i)$, retained set
+$\mathcal R=\{1,\dots,k\}$ and $\lambda_k>\lambda_{k+1}$; let $E_{ji}=q_j^{T}Eq_i$;
+and let $P,\hat P$ be the projectors, in the whitened coordinates of the true
+$\Sigma$, onto the subspaces fitted with $\Sigma$ and with $\hat\Sigma$.  Then
+$\hat P=P+\delta P+O(\varepsilon^{2})$ with
+\begin{equation}
+    q_j^{T}\,\delta P\,q_i=-\frac{\lambda_j E_{ji}}{\lambda_i-\lambda_j}
+    \qquad(i\in\mathcal R,\ j\notin\mathcal R),
+    \label{eq:mlst-local-displacement}
+\end{equation}
+and every other entry of $\delta P$ zero.  Consequently: \emph{(1)} only the
+coupling block $E_{\mathcal R^{\perp}\mathcal R}$ rotates the subspace to
+first order --- the within-retained and within-discarded blocks deform
+coordinates and rotate nothing; \emph{(2)} each coupling entry is weighted by
+the discarded eigenvalue and divided by its \emph{own} pairwise gap, not by
+the global gap, so
+$\|\delta P\|_F^{2}=2\sum_{i\in\mathcal R,j\notin\mathcal R}\lambda_j^{2}E_{ji}^{2}/(\lambda_i-\lambda_j)^{2}$;
+\emph{(3)} if $E$ commutes with $A$ (entries $e_i>-1$) the statement is exact
+at every order --- the eigenvectors are unchanged, the eigenvalues become
+$\lambda_i/(1+e_i)$, and the fitted subspace equals the oracle one until the
+perturbed spectrum reorders, at which point the retained set is exchanged;
+\emph{(4)} if the estimator diagonalizes a sample operator $\hat A=A+D$, the
+joint first-order displacement from the population subspace is
+$(D_{ji}-\lambda_j E_{ji})/(\lambda_i-\lambda_j)$ --- the training-sample and
+covariance terms add and are separated by the same gaps.  Expanding instead in
+the eigenbasis of $\hat A$, at fixed training data, removes $D$ and gives
+\eqref{eq:mlst-local-displacement} with $(\hat\lambda_i,\hat q_i)$; that is
+the form the controlled test of Section~\ref{subsec:mlst-cov-anatomy} checks.
+The remainder is of order $\varepsilon^{2}\lambda_1(A)/\delta$ with
+$\delta=\min_{i\in\mathcal R,j\notin\mathcal R}|\lambda_i-\lambda_j|$.
+\end{proposition}
+
+The expansion requires $I+E\succ0$.  A near-singular $\hat\Sigma$,
+$\lambda_{\min}(I+E)\to0$, is \emph{outside} it, and outside the global bound
+as well; Section~\ref{subsec:mlst-cov-anatomy} reports that regime
+separately.  A covariance model wrong in \emph{structure} rather than merely
+noisy need not have a large $\varepsilon$ ---
+$\hat\Sigma=\Sigma^{1/2}(I+\delta vv^{T})\Sigma^{1/2}$ is a covariance with
+$\varepsilon=|\delta|$ --- but its $\varepsilon$ does not vanish as the
+calibration sample grows: the estimate converges to the misspecified model.
+That case is what the GWOSC stage probes.  The proof is in \sup{S7}.
+
+\subsection{A structural prior trades approximation for estimation}
+\label{subsec:mlst-structural-prior-theory}
+
+Holding the metric fixed, the fourth class \emph{restricts} the learned
+subspace: for a two-axis measurement $Z\in\mathbb{C}^{C\times T}$ with
+separable noise $\Sigma=\Sigma_c\otimes\Sigma_t$, the noise-factored
+projection autoencoder (NFPA) admits only $\mathcal{S}_c\otimes\mathcal{S}_t$.
+The restricted bridge (\sup{S8}) says it solves the same objective over a
+product Grassmannian, with equality to the unrestricted optimum exactly when
+that optimum is separable; at $(k_c,k_t)=(1,1)$ with a separable template it
+degenerates to the joint-channel optimal filter.  The separable class matters
+because it is the first at which the \emph{architecture}, not the metric,
+decides what can be represented.
+
+Its cost is measurable, and the following identity is what a finite-sample
+class contrast actually measures.
+
+\begin{proposition}[Exact risk decomposition and a lower bound on the restriction cost]
+\label{prop:mlst-risk-decomposition}
+Let $K\succeq0$ be the whitened clean-signal second moment with eigenvalues
+$\mu_1\ge\mu_2\ge\cdots$; for a rank-$k$ projector $P$ ($k=k_ck_t$) let
+$R_K(P)=\operatorname{tr}K-\operatorname{tr}(PK)$; let $P^{\star}_U$ be the
+top-$k$ eigenprojector of $K$ with gap $\Delta_k=\mu_k-\mu_{k+1}$; let
+$\mathcal F$ be the compact set of product projectors and
+$P^{\star}_{\mathcal F}\in\arg\min_{\mathcal F}R_K$.  Then for any fitted
+$\hat P_{\mathcal F}\in\mathcal F$ and any fitted rank-$k$ projector $\hat P_U$,
+\begin{equation}
+    R_K(\hat P_{\mathcal F})-R_K(\hat P_U)
+    =\underbrace{R_K(P^{\star}_{\mathcal F})-R_K(P^{\star}_U)}_{a\ \ge\ 0}
+    +\underbrace{R_K(\hat P_{\mathcal F})-R_K(P^{\star}_{\mathcal F})}_{x_{\mathcal F}\ \ge\ 0}
+    -\underbrace{R_K(\hat P_U)-R_K(P^{\star}_U)}_{x_U\ \ge\ 0},
+    \label{eq:mlst-risk-decomposition}
+\end{equation}
+an identity, and for every $P_F\in\mathcal F$
+\begin{equation}
+    R_K(P_F)-R_K(P^{\star}_U)\ \ge\ \Delta_k\bigl(k-\operatorname{tr}(P_FP^{\star}_U)\bigr),
+    \quad
+    \operatorname{tr}(P_FP^{\star}_U)\le\min\Bigl\{k,\ \textstyle\sum_{i\le k_c}\mu_i(M_c),\ \sum_{i\le k_t}\mu_i(M_t)\Bigr\},
+    \label{eq:mlst-restriction-bound}
+\end{equation}
+where $M_c$ and $M_t$ are the partial traces of $P^{\star}_U$ over the
+temporal and channel factors.  Hence $a$ is bounded below by a quantity
+computed from $K$ alone, and when $\Delta_k>0$ it vanishes if and only if
+$P^{\star}_U\in\mathcal F$.
+\end{proposition}
+
+The bound is what makes the sign of the first term a fact about the generator
+rather than about the fits.  Two cautions govern its use.  An observed signed
+contrast between held-out weighted residuals at matched rank is a
+\emph{finite-sample class contrast}: it mixes $a$ with the two estimation
+excesses, and it can be negative --- the restriction acting as a regularizer
+at finite $N$ --- without the population approximation error being negative,
+which it cannot be.  And agreement of the objective across random restarts
+bounds the optimization term \emph{only for the restarts tried}; it does not
+certify a global optimum.  Section~\ref{subsec:mlst-structural-prior} reports
+both terms under those names.  The proof is in \sup{S9}.
