+\section{Estimator instances}
+\label{sec:mlst-estimators}
+
+\subsection{Optimal filtering as the rank-1 case}
+\label{subsec:mlst-of}
+
+Fixing $\hat s=\alpha p$ for a known template $p$ turns \eqref{eq:mlst-chi2}
+into a one-parameter generalized least-squares problem with the closed-form
+minimizer and variance
+\begin{equation}
+    \hat\alpha=\frac{p^{\dagger}\Sigma^{-1}x}{p^{\dagger}\Sigma^{-1}p},
+    \qquad
+    \operatorname{Var}(\hat\alpha)=\frac{1}{p^{\dagger}\Sigma^{-1}p},
+    \label{eq:mlst-of}
+\end{equation}
+which attains the Cram\'er--Rao bound for the amplitude under the assumed
+model.  For stationary noise with one-sided PSD $J$, the kernel of
+\eqref{eq:mlst-of} is the familiar $\tilde p^{*}_k/J_k$ matched filter and the
+resolution is $\sigma_E^{-2}=\sum_k|\tilde p_k|^{2}/J_k$.  Time-shift and
+joint-channel OF are the same projection with an enlarged fixed class
+(\sup{S10}); the estimator is unbiased only if the true response lies in
+$\operatorname{span}\{p\}$, and template mismatch biases it by the component
+of the response orthogonal to $p$ in the $\Sigma^{-1}$ metric --- the
+limitation the learned classes remove.
+
+\paragraph{The token-axis principle.}
+For wide-sense stationary noise periodic with the record length, $\Sigma$ is
+circulant and $\Sigma^{-1}=F^{\dagger}\operatorname{diag}(1/J_k)F$: the
+eigenvectors of the precision operator \emph{are} the discrete Fourier modes
+and its eigenvalues are the inverse-PSD weights of the OF kernel.  An encoding
+of the time axis by the leading eigenvectors of $\Sigma^{-1}$, weighted by
+their eigenvalues, is therefore the matched-filter weighting itself.  This is
+the same construction that positional encodings for attention arrive at from
+the other side, where the operator is an \emph{assumed} graph Laplacian
+\citep{bronstein2021geometric,dwivedi2020generalization,vaswani2017attention};
+here it is the \emph{measured} noise precision.  Three qualifications keep the
+statement where it belongs.  The precision weights $1/J_k$ are not the
+whitening scalings $1/\sqrt{J_k}$: the two operators share the eigenbasis, and
+the proposition identifies a basis and its weights, not a preferred encoding.
+Nothing here concerns a learned model --- a learned query--key projection
+changes the geometry attention computes, so no statement about attention
+scores follows from prewhitening inputs.  And on a finite non-periodic window
+the covariance is Toeplitz, asymptotically equivalent to its circulant
+counterpart in normalized Hilbert--Schmidt norm but \emph{not} in operator
+norm \citep{gray2006toeplitz}, so an inverse-covariance-weighted statistic on
+a finite window is not uniformly approximated by its circulant version: the
+exact statements here need the periodic model.
+
+\subsection{Covariance-weighted subspaces}
+\label{subsec:mlst-empca}
+
+Letting $\hat s$ range over a learned rank-$k$ subspace gives
+\begin{equation}
+    \min_{U,\,\{c_i\}}\ \frac1N\sum_i(x_i-Uc_i)^{\dagger}\Sigma^{-1}(x_i-Uc_i),
+    \label{eq:mlst-rankk}
+\end{equation}
+whose solution in whitened coordinates is the top-$k$ SVD subspace of
+$\Sigma^{-1/2}X$ --- a classical reduction \citep{gabriel1979lower,allen2014gls}
+implemented here by the EMPCA alternation \citep{bailey2012principal}.  Two
+population statements follow.  At $k=1$ with a unique leading whitened signal
+eigendirection and nonzero eigengap, weighted PCA recovers the OF direction;
+at finite sample the degeneracy is exact whenever the fitted rank-1 basis
+spans the template, in which case the two estimators coincide identically, not
+merely in expectation.  At $k>1$ containment is \emph{not} automatic: the
+learned subspace need not contain the template, and the OF--EMPCA comparison
+therefore tests the benefit of enlarging the class, while the same-rank
+PCA--EMPCA comparison tests the contribution of the geometry.  We keep the two
+apart throughout, because a gain over a fixed template is not by itself
+evidence that inverse-covariance weighting caused it.
+
+The subspace is determined only up to the gauge $U\mapsto UQ$; latent
+coordinates are not observables, and every quantity reported below is a
+function of the projector or of the reconstruction.  Finite-sample stability
+is governed by the whitened spectral gap through a Davis--Kahan argument, made
+local and quantitative by Proposition~\ref{prop:mlst-local-displacement}.
+Rank selection is not part of the theory: rank is fixed by the protocol in
+every experiment below and is never selected on the evaluation data.
+
+\subsection{Tied and untied autoencoders}
+\label{subsec:mlst-ae}
+
+Writing the same projection as an encoder and a decoder makes the connection
+to representation learning explicit.  With decoder $P$ and encoder
+$P^{\dagger}\Sigma^{-1}$ --- the $\Sigma^{-1}$-adjoint of the decoder, which
+is the tying condition --- the reconstruction is $PP^{\dagger}\Sigma^{-1}x$ and
+Theorem~\ref{thm:mlst-bridge} identifies its optimum with EMPCA's.  Untying
+the encoder leaves the optimal reconstruction and the class unchanged; it
+changes the parameterization and the optimization landscape, and in a
+controlled comparison at fixed budget the untied arm needed more iterations
+without finding a better optimum (\sup{S11}).  The practical consequence for
+loss design is direct: a noise-aware autoencoder trained under
+\eqref{eq:mlst-chi2} is not a heuristic reweighting but the Gaussian
+likelihood of the assumed detector model, and its optimum is a classical
+weighted-PCA subspace.
+
+\subsection{The separable class and its alternation}
+\label{subsec:mlst-nfpa}
+
+For $Z\in\mathbb{C}^{C\times T}$ with $\Sigma=\Sigma_c\otimes\Sigma_t$,
+per-axis whitening $\tilde Z=\Sigma_c^{-1/2}Z\,\Sigma_t^{-\dagger/2}$ turns
+the objective into a Frobenius one, and NFPA restricts the reconstruction to
+$U_cU_c^{\dagger}\tilde Z\,U_tU_t^{\dagger}$ with orthonormal factors.  The
+restricted bridge makes this the same objective over
+$\mathrm{Gr}(k_c,C)\times\mathrm{Gr}(k_t,T)$, solved by alternating per-axis
+eigendecompositions --- multilinear PCA \citep{lu2008mpca} in the whitened
+metric.  The parameter count drops from $k(CT-k)$ to
+$k_c(C-k_c)+k_t(T-k_t)$, which is the source of the estimation saving that
+Proposition~\ref{prop:mlst-risk-decomposition} sets against the approximation
+cost.  The alternation is not convex: agreement across restarts bounds the
+optimization term only for the restarts tried, and every claim below that
+depends on it is labelled \emph{best-found} rather than optimal.  Gauge and
+update details are in \sup{S12}.
