+\section{Controlled evidence}
+\label{sec:mlst-controlled}
+
+\subsection{Conventions}
+\label{subsec:mlst-conventions}
+
+Every study below was predeclared, executed once, and is reported with the
+registered predictions that were \emph{not} met.  Rank is fixed by the
+protocol and never selected on evaluation data; splits are chronological or
+seed-paired as stated; intervals are bootstrap unless named otherwise, and a
+95\% interval is reported beside every contrast.  The programme file, the
+protocol JSONs and the per-task fit caps are archived, and every number traces
+to a checksummed record (Section~\ref{sec:mlst-availability}).  The full
+verification grid, per-cell tables and diagnostics are in \sup{S13--S16}.
+
+A suite of nine frozen regression gates (S1--S9) verifies the estimator
+identities rather than the paper's scientific claims: OF normalization against
+the Cram\'er--Rao bound gave $0.994$ $[0.985,1.002]$; an independently trained
+tied linear autoencoder reached the EMPCA objective with mean relative gap
+$1.95\times10^{-16}$, confirming Theorem~\ref{thm:mlst-bridge} numerically;
+and under white noise PCA and EMPCA differed by $1.48\times10^{-6}$ degrees in
+maximum principal angle, which is prediction (P1) and the arccos precision
+floor.  The colored-noise gate S5 is (P2) and (P3) on one generator: over
+30~seeds the paired PCA-minus-EMPCA held-out weighted residual was $+0.550$
+$[0.530,0.571]$ and the held-out negative log-likelihood $+11.67$
+$[11.24,12.11]$, both favouring EMPCA, while \emph{raw MSE preferred the
+opposite reconstruction} ($2330$ against $7413$) and the two subspaces were
+nearly orthogonal in the whitened metric ($86.25^{\circ}$
+$[85.16,87.21]$).  The same run archives the Euclidean clean-signal error,
+which favoured EMPCA in all thirty seeds ($58.2$ against $5110.9$); by
+Example~\ref{ex:mlst-risk-counterexample} that agreement is a property of this
+generator, not a guarantee.
+
+\subsection{Covariance error: what actually moves the subspace}
+\label{subsec:mlst-cov-anatomy}
+
+Two studies on one frozen generator ($d=256$, exponent $-1$ power-law PSD,
+rank-2 signal at SNR~3 and $3/2$, $N=512$, $\lambda_1/\Delta_\lambda=3.74$)
+ask how a wrong covariance moves the learned subspace.
+
+The first (T15, 80~fits) varies the \emph{source} of the error --- calibration
+sample size $m$, or structured misspecification of the PSD exponent and
+correlation length at fixed trace --- and compares the rotation against the
+\emph{paired oracle fit on the same training draw}, because the bound governs
+the effect of handing the estimator a perturbed covariance, not the distance
+to the planted subspace (which the oracle arm itself misses by
+$\sin\Theta=0.32$ from training-sample error alone).  The global bound held on
+all 80~fits, but its hypothesis $\varepsilon\le1/2$ holds only for the oracle
+arm and the two largest calibration sizes, and \emph{where the theorem applies
+it is uninformative}: $6\varepsilon\lambda_1/\Delta_\lambda$ is $8.6$ and
+$4.3$, above the value $1$ that $\sin\Theta$ cannot exceed, and by the fitted
+scaling $\varepsilon\approx0.59\sqrt{d/m}$ an informative value would need
+$m\approx4.5\times10^{4}$ calibration traces at this $d$.  The instructive
+comparison is between two arms of nearly equal $\varepsilon$: the structured
+correlation-length misspecification ($\varepsilon=0.557$) is nearly harmless
+($\sin\Theta=0.012$; amplitude-probe $R^{2}$ $0.921$ against the oracle's
+$0.922$), while an empirical covariance from $m=256=d$ traces
+($\varepsilon=0.575$) is catastrophic ($\sin\Theta=0.94$, $R^{2}=0.11$).
+Recovery is well under way at $m=2d$ ($R^{2}=0.83$) and near complete at
+$m=8d$ ($0.91$).  An upper bound in $\varepsilon$ never implied that
+perturbations of equal $\varepsilon$ act alike; what this shows is that
+$\varepsilon$ is an \emph{incomplete summary}.
+
+The second study (T19, 38~arms, ten seeds, 380~fits) tests the local statement
+that explains why.  Because the fit diagonalizes the \emph{sample} whitened
+operator, the perturbation families were engineered in the sample eigenbasis
+at a fixed training draw, so the training-sample term drops out and the
+displacement isolates the covariance term.  Three regimes appeared, and
+Figure~\ref{fig:mlst-cov-anatomy} collects them.
+\emph{Coupling.}  On the designed coupling, random-symmetric and
+stationary/Toeplitz families at $\varepsilon\le0.2$, the measured Frobenius
+displacement was within a factor of two of the first-order value of
+Proposition~\ref{prop:mlst-local-displacement} on 70~of~70 fits (medians
+$0.98$--$1.02$), with residuals scaling as $\varepsilon^{2.05}$ for a generic
+$E$ and $\varepsilon^{2.9}$ for a pure coupling block, whose second-order term
+vanishes identically.  The registered slope prediction for the coupling family
+is \emph{not} something the theory implies, and it was scored not met.
+\emph{Separation.}  A diagonal $E$ preserving the eigenvalue ordering left the
+subspace unchanged ($\sin\Theta\le4\times10^{-8}$ on 40~of~40 fits up to
+$\varepsilon=0.4$), and an order-reversing one exchanged the retained subspace
+exactly when the \emph{sample} spectrum check flipped (30~of~30; sample
+threshold $0.35$--$0.43$ at $d/N=1/2$, against the population value $0.72$).
+\emph{Conditioning} is a separate regime, outside both the proposition and the
+global bound.  An empirical covariance from $m\le d$ traces has
+$\lambda_{\min}(I+E)<0.05$ on all 20~fits; at $m=d$ the subspace is destroyed
+($\sin\Theta=0.99$), at $m=d/2$ with a uniform eigenvalue floor it is only
+partially lost (median $0.73$), and NLL-selected shrinkage restores
+$\lambda_{\min}$ to $0.21$--$0.35$ while leaving $\sin\Theta$ between $0.21$
+and $0.99$: the regularizer repairs the spectrum floor, not the direction of
+the sampling error.
+
+This is a \emph{conditional} check at fixed training data with engineered
+perturbations, not a test of an independently estimated covariance, and it is
+a local theorem check, not a finite-perturbation guarantee.  A
+finite-perturbation bound of this form remains open.
+
+\begin{figure}[!htbp]
+    \centering
+    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{generated/figures/fig_cov_anatomy.pdf}
+    \caption{Covariance-perturbation anatomy (T19; ten seeds per arm,
+    $d=256$, $k=2$, $N=512$; displacement measured against the paired oracle
+    fit on the same training draw).  (a)~Measured Frobenius displacement of
+    the fitted projector against the first-order value of
+    Proposition~\ref{prop:mlst-local-displacement} for perturbations built in
+    the sample eigenbasis: a pure coupling block (C), a full random symmetric
+    $E$ (F), and estimated/misspecified covariances (M); filled markers are
+    the primary band $\varepsilon\le0.2$, the band is a factor of two about
+    the identity.  (b)~Diagonal $E$: ordering-preserving perturbations (P)
+    leave the subspace unchanged at every $\varepsilon$ tested;
+    order-reversing ones (R) exchange it exactly at the sample-spectrum
+    reversal (shaded: per-seed sample thresholds; dashed: the population
+    value).  (c)~Conditioning: subspace error against $\lambda_{\min}(I+E)$
+    for the near-singular empirical covariances (consistent decoder, v2), the
+    shrinkage estimates, and the well-conditioned arms.  Registered inputs and
+    field names: \nolinkurl{generated/SOURCE_MAP_part2.md}.}
+    \label{fig:mlst-cov-anatomy}
+\end{figure}
+
+\subsection{A structural prior at finite sample}
+\label{subsec:mlst-structural-prior}
+
+Proposition~\ref{prop:mlst-risk-decomposition} says what the separable
+restriction costs and buys.  Two studies on a planted two-axis family measure
+it.
+
+T14 mapped the finite-sample class contrast at $C=8$, $T=256$,
+$(k_c,k_t)=(2,3)$ with the oracle covariance, over
+$N\in\{200,\dots,3200\}$ and $\alpha\in\{0,0.4,0.8\}$ (25~cells, 1000~fits).
+The contrast was negative in every cell at SNR~10 --- the restriction helping
+--- with paired intervals excluding zero in fourteen of fifteen, and
+indistinguishable from zero at $\alpha=0.8$, SNR~3.  \emph{No crossover was
+found anywhere on the grid}, so the predeclared confirmation stage was not
+triggered and the original gate --- a transition within the grid --- was not
+met.  The null is not explained by the exploration itself, but the population
+approximation cost at $\alpha>0$ was never established independently of the
+fits, so the follow-up measured it directly.
+
+T20 carried out the population accounting at $T=32$ ($d=256$), same ranks and
+SNR~10, with $K$ estimated from $2\times10^{5}$ clean events per $\alpha$.
+The unrestricted reference is the top-six eigenprojector of $K$; the
+restricted reference is the \emph{best of 51} alternating least-squares
+solutions on $K$, all agreeing to $10^{-13}$ --- restart agreement, not a
+certificate.  The resulting cost $a$ is $-7\times10^{-15}$ at $\alpha=0$,
+where the product family contains the excited signal exactly (the planted
+rank-six class excites only four directions there, because the coefficient map
+ties two entries), and $2.71$ and $2.82$ at $\alpha=0.4$ and $0.8$, about
+$3\%$ of $\operatorname{tr}K$.  The lower bound \eqref{eq:mlst-restriction-bound},
+computed from the gap and the partial traces on the same $K$, gives $0.10$ and
+$0.55$: \emph{the cost is provably positive at both nonzero $\alpha$}, and the
+true optimum lies between the bound and the best-found value.  Both apply to
+the archived Monte-Carlo covariance proxy, not to an exactly known population.
+
+Over $N\in\{50,\dots,1600\}$ (180~paired comparisons) the identity holds by
+construction, $x_U>x_{\mathcal F}$ throughout with fitted constants
+$c_U/c_F$ between 3 and 5, and the \emph{registered $c/N$ form is refuted}:
+the unrestricted excess decays with effective slopes $N^{-0.49}$ and
+$N^{-0.38}$, and the crossover those constants forecast near $N=160$ is not
+there.  On fresh seeds $10$--$19$, chosen after the exploration and evaluated
+once, the contrast is negative at $N=800$ ($-0.39$ $[-0.49,-0.29]$ and $-0.29$
+$[-0.40,-0.18]$) and positive at $N=1600$ ($+0.90$ $[+0.83,+0.96]$ and $+0.87$
+$[+0.80,+0.94]$) at $\alpha=0.4$ and $0.8$.  At $C=8$, $T=32$ and SNR~10 the
+restricted learner therefore wins at $N=800$ and the unrestricted one at
+$N=1600$ for both tested nonseparabilities; at $\alpha=0$, where $a=0$, it is
+negative at every $N$.  An exploratory log-linear interpolation places the
+sign change near $N=960$; that is interpolation, the registered $1/N$
+extrapolation did not predict it, and $N^{\star}$ is \emph{bracketed, not
+located}.  The T14 null at $T=256$ stands as a null: the two configurations
+differ in dimension and in the pulse family, and the whitened spectra at the
+three $\alpha$ were not matched as the protocol asked, so within-$\alpha$
+comparisons across $N$ are the evidence.  Restart selection by training loss
+matched the population-best restart in $50$--$90\%$ of fits, with spreads up
+to $0.18$ --- of the order of the contrast near the sign change --- so the
+optimization term is visible exactly where the classes are closest.
+
+\begin{figure}[!htbp]
+    \centering
+    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{generated/figures/fig_structural_prior_crossover.pdf}
+    \caption{Structural prior at finite sample (T20; planted two-axis family,
+    $C=8$, $T=32$, $(k_c,k_t)=(2,3)$, SNR~10, oracle covariance).
+    (a)~Clean-signal risk contrast between the fitted separable and
+    unrestricted subspaces against $N$: exploration seeds $0$--$9$ with
+    seed-paired 95\% intervals, and fresh seeds $10$--$19$ evaluated once
+    (open diamonds).  (b)~The terms of
+    Proposition~\ref{prop:mlst-risk-decomposition}: the estimation excesses
+    $x_U$ (solid) and $x_{\mathcal F}$ (dashed), the best-found approximation
+    cost $a$ (solid horizontal) and its provable lower bound
+    \eqref{eq:mlst-restriction-bound} (dotted).  The contrast changes sign
+    where $x_U-x_{\mathcal F}$ falls through $a$.  Registered inputs:
+    \nolinkurl{generated/SOURCE_MAP_part2.md}.}
+    \label{fig:mlst-structural-prior}
+\end{figure}
+
+\subsection{Excited is not detectable}
+\label{subsec:mlst-excitation}
+
+Section~\ref{subsec:mlst-pullback} covers two cases --- a direction the
+training data excite and one they do not.  T21 scaled the excitation of one
+physical latent continuously between them (planted family at $\alpha=0$,
+$C=8$, $T=256$, oracle covariance, SNR~10, $s\in\{0,0.03,0.1,0.3\}$ with the
+others at~1, plus three controls; 19~cells, ten seeds, 190~closed-form rank-4
+fits) and asked what sets recovery $\|Pu_j\|^{2}/\|u_j\|^{2}$ of the whitened
+Jacobian column $u_j$.
+
+\emph{None of the five registered predictions was met as registered.}
+Recovery is not a function of the excitation ratio alone.  At $s=0$ recovery
+is $0.13$--$0.37$ for three of four directions against a geometric chance of
+$0.002$, because the planted directions are not orthogonal (Gram entries to
+$-0.65$); the $s=0$ probe returning the prior is a constant-target artefact,
+not evidence about the representation.  The weak-regime variance signature
+failed under its registered baseline, the $0.95$ strong-excitation level was
+not reached at $N=800$ and only two directions reached it at $N=3200$, and the
+across-seed recovery variance is \emph{peaked} at the detection edge rather
+than monotone.
+
+An exploratory reading --- recorded for an independent test, post hoc, and
+claimed as nothing more --- is that recovery is set by the spike strengths of
+the population signal covariance relative to $d/N$ and by each direction's
+loadings on the population eigenvectors: the fourth population spike, $0.54$
+above the noise floor, lies below $\sqrt{d/N}$ at both $N$, and the
+isolated-spike overlap combined with those loadings reproduces all eight
+observed full-excitation recoveries to within $0.01$.  The grid lesson is
+recorded too: $s\in\{0.03,0.1\}$ are near-replicas of $s=0$ because the
+induced variance scales as $s^{2}$, so a successor should grid on eigen-spike
+strength, keep the loadings, measure each direction's unique component, use a
+frozen readout and include a genuine all-zero control.
+\emph{Excited and detectable are different properties of a planted direction},
+and no abstention threshold for any downstream diagnostic is established here.
+
+\begin{figure}[!htbp]
+    \centering
+    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{generated/figures/fig_excitation_detectability.pdf}
+    \caption{Excitation and detectability (T21; planted family at $\alpha=0$,
+    $C=8$, $T=256$, rank four, oracle covariance, ten seeds per cell).
+    (a)~Seed-mean recovery of each planted direction against the Rayleigh
+    quotient of the training-distribution signal covariance along it, in
+    excess of the noise floor and relative to $\sqrt{d/N}$; recovery is not a
+    function of this scalar.  (b)~\emph{Post hoc, not predeclared}: recovery
+    predicted by the isolated-spike overlap weighted by each direction's
+    squared eigenvector loadings, against the observed seed mean.  Agreement
+    is within $0.015$ on every point; it is a description of these runs, and
+    its test on fresh data is left open.  Registered inputs:
+    \nolinkurl{generated/SOURCE_MAP_part2.md}.}
+    \label{fig:mlst-excitation}
+\end{figure}
+
