+\section{Discussion}
+\label{sec:mlst-discussion}
+
+The theory and the evidence support one organizing statement: under the stated
+linear Gaussian model a reconstruction method is the pair
+$(\Sigma^{-1},\mathcal{F})$ of a residual metric fixed by the measured noise and
+an admissible class fixed by the architecture.  Once the metric is fixed, the
+four classes here are not competing likelihoods but different feasible sets for
+one $\Sigma^{-1}$-orthogonal projection, and the separable class is the first at
+which the architecture rather than the metric decides what can be represented.
+The equivalences are deterministic identities between optimization problems,
+exact under matched preprocessing, weighting and normalization; the noise model
+enters only in choosing the weight.
+
+What this version adds is what happens to that statement when the covariance is
+estimated and the sample is finite, and the answers are more specific --- and
+more negative --- than the identities alone suggest.
+
+\emph{The metric is only as good as the covariance supplied, and sensitivity is
+not a function of the error's size.}
+Proposition~\ref{prop:mlst-local-displacement} locates it in the coupling block
+of the relative error between retained and discarded eigenspaces, divided by the
+pairwise gaps, and the controlled study found the three regimes the proposition
+separates: a first-order regime in which the displacement follows the coupling
+block within a factor of two; an ordering regime in which a diagonal error of
+any size leaves the subspace untouched until the spectrum reorders and then
+exchanges it; and a conditioning regime --- near-singular empirical covariance
+--- that no perturbation statement here covers and that shrinkage repairs only
+in its spectrum.  This is why two perturbations of equal relative norm differed
+by two orders of magnitude in effect, and why the global norm bound, valid
+wherever its hypothesis held, was uninformative there.  A finite-perturbation
+statement of the same shape remains open.
+
+\emph{The class trades approximation for estimation, and the trade is
+measurable but not predictable from the registered model.}
+Proposition~\ref{prop:mlst-risk-decomposition} separates the finite-sample
+class contrast into a population approximation cost --- provably positive off
+the separable case --- and two estimation excesses, and the accounting observed
+both regimes on one configuration, with the crossover bracketed between $N=800$
+and $N=1600$ on fresh seeds.  The registered $c/N$ scaling of the estimation
+excesses was \emph{refuted}, and its forecast crossover was wrong by a factor of
+six; the interpolated $N^{\star}\approx960$ is interpolation, not measurement.
+
+\emph{Excitation in training does not make a physical direction recoverable.}
+All five registered predictions of the excitation study failed.  What the
+exploratory reading suggests --- that the induced population spectrum and the
+direction's eigenvector loadings set recovery --- is recorded as a hypothesis
+for an independent test and supplies no operational threshold for any
+downstream diagnostic.
+
+\emph{On real data the boundary is where the evidence is.}  CRESST gives a
+positive same-rank result under an assumed covariance it cannot validate;
+GWOSC shows that the assumed covariance can fail calibration badly while every
+implementation check passes; TIDMAD supplies recorded truth and reports no
+useful whole-window recovery at the frozen operating point, with a
+dimension-to-sample ratio of 187 as the leading hypothesis.
+Section~\ref{subsec:mlst-t22} tests that hypothesis directly.
+
+\paragraph{Untested design suggestions.}
+Two follow from Section~\ref{subsec:mlst-of} and are recorded as suggestions,
+not results: prewhiten before tokenization, and tokenize along the axis in
+which $\Sigma^{-1}$ is diagonal.  Neither is tested here.  Whitening supplies
+no locality, no graph structure and no deformation stability; those are
+properties of the detector domain, not of the noise model.
+
+\section{Limitations}
+\label{sec:mlst-limitations}
+
+\emph{Scope of the identities.}  They concern linear subspaces and Gaussian
+noise with a known or independently estimated positive-definite covariance.
+They do not cover curved signal manifolds, saturation, pile-up, heavy-tailed
+contamination, or covariance estimated from the very event being reconstructed.
+Timing variation can be approximated locally by extra linear directions, but a
+finite subspace need not represent large shifts.  A Mahalanobis objective is
+efficient only when its noise model is adequate: covariance drift or outliers
+can invalidate its uncertainty interpretation even when the optimization is
+correct.
+
+\emph{Identifiability of noise components.}  An eigendecomposition of a
+covariance produces decorrelated statistical modes, not uniquely labelled
+physical sources.  Nothing here recovers a physical noise decomposition from a
+measured total spectrum, and no result should be read as attributing residual
+improvement to a named noise mechanism.
+
+\emph{CRESST.}  Seven run/channel clusters from two runs, one operating point,
+one rank, one shrinkage.  The primary predeclared interval resamples 19{,}335
+pooled events and quantifies event-level uncertainty \emph{conditional on these
+channels}; it does not quantify variation from sampling a new channel.  The
+post-freeze channel-then-event analysis retains the effect directions but its
+intervals are coarse with seven clusters.  One negative-control exception
+(wrong-PSD weighting on \texttt{35/58.0}) shows that pooled control behaviour
+need not hold per channel.  The trigger-efficiency calculation was underpowered
+over the frozen amplitude grid --- six channels inconclusive at the primary 5\%
+false-positive rate, none reaching 90\% efficiency --- so reconstruction gains
+cannot be translated into a threshold, an exposure or a sensitivity
+improvement.  Rank/shrinkage robustness and time-cut sensitivity are
+\emph{deferred}, not established.
+
+\emph{GWOSC.}  One 256-second interval, one event region, two PSD models.  The
+shared-FIR result says two software paths agree when they implement the same
+finite-duration statistic; it does not establish equivalence between the
+PSD-domain GLS statistic and a finite FIR one.  Agreement with GWpy isolates
+one implementation question and cannot make a Gaussian PSD model adequate for
+nonstationary real noise.
+
+\emph{TIDMAD.}  Named validation files at named operating points, not a survey
+of the release.  The whole-window stage established \emph{no} useful recovery
+and did not confirm the rank-2 prediction on any real file; the linear
+estimator is not competitive on the release's own detector-facing denoising
+score.  The narrowband stage is a temporal hold-out on files already examined
+--- new time regions of previously used files --- and is \emph{not}
+independent-file replication.  Its dependence assessment leaves nine complete
+blocks at the largest block length, the same limiting count as the whole-window
+stage, and the declared rule withholds every benefit verdict when that
+assessment does not stabilise.  The calibration model is checked at second
+moments only: no tails, no dependence, no coverage.
+
+\emph{Controlled studies.}  One generator per study, oracle or engineered
+covariance, fixed rank.  The covariance study is conditional on a fixed
+training draw with engineered perturbations; it is a local theorem check, not a
+finite-perturbation guarantee, and it says nothing about an independently
+estimated covariance in deployment.  The structural-prior accounting uses a
+Monte-Carlo covariance proxy, and its restricted reference is the best of 51
+alternating-least-squares starts --- restart agreement, not a certified global
+optimum.  Restart spreads are of the order of the contrast near the sign
+change, so the optimization term is visible exactly where the classes are
+closest.  The excitation study's spectrum-and-loadings reading is post hoc,
+checked on the runs that suggested it.
+\ifgatedevidence
+The tied-versus-untied ablation concerns one colored configuration and a white
+control under one optimizer with a fixed budget.
+\else
+The tied-versus-untied ablation and the July excited-support experiment belong
+to the held evidence (Section~\ref{sec:mlst-intro}) and are not reported here.
+\fi
+
+\emph{Not claimed anywhere.}  Detection sensitivity, energy resolution,
+false-alarm probability, a universal recovery threshold, an operational
+abstention rule, exact restricted population optima, or a validated $c/N$ law.
+
+\section{Conclusion}
+\label{sec:mlst-conclusion}
+
+Under a linear Gaussian measurement model the inverse noise covariance is the
+metric in which a detector reconstruction is learned; the architecture fixes
+only the admissible class, and the training data only the excited directions.
+Optimal filtering, covariance-weighted PCA, tied noise-aware linear
+autoencoders and their Kronecker-separable restriction are one projection in
+that metric.  The residual it minimizes predicts the whitened, not the
+Euclidean, clean-signal error.  At finite sample the metric's sensitivity to
+covariance error is local --- a coupling block divided by pairwise gaps, with
+a separate conditioning regime that no perturbation bound covers --- the
+class's structural prior trades a provably positive approximation cost against
+an estimation saving whose crossover we bracket but cannot predict, and
+excitation in training is not detectability.  On public detector data the
+weighting lowers every held-out weighted residual on seven CRESST channels; a
+Gaussian PSD model fails calibration on GWOSC while every implementation check
+passes; and against recorded TIDMAD truth no rank-2 whole-window reconstruction
+beats predicting zero.  The determinants are separable, and each of them can
+fail on its own.
+
+\section{Code and data availability}
+\label{sec:mlst-availability}
+
+All results come from public datasets; no internal or unreleased detector data,
+noise budget or instrument simulation is used or described.  The CRESST
+pulse-shape release is described in Ref.~\citep{angloher2025cresstpulshape} and
+hosted by the ORIGINS Dark Matter Data Center; the collaboration asks that the
+description and Ref.~\citep{angloher2023datacleaning} be cited, and we do so.
+The GWOSC strain is the O1 bulk release \citep{gwoscgw150914,abbott2021opendata},
+CC~BY~4.0; this research has made use of data obtained from the Gravitational
+Wave Open Science Center, a service of the LIGO Scientific Collaboration, the
+Virgo Collaboration and KAGRA.  The TIDMAD data are the public release under
+CC-BY-4.0 \citep{fry2024tidmad}.
+
+Every quantitative claim in this paper is listed in the claim-to-evidence table
+of the accompanying record, with its mathematical assumption, dataset and
+split, the exact output field it is read from, the code revision, the archived
+output path, and its limitation.  The archived evidence is versioned with a
+complete SHA-256 manifest and is verified by a single command; figures and
+tables are regenerated from it by scripts that read every value from a
+registered file and hard-code none.  Preregistrations, protocol JSONs, fit caps
+and the deviations decided before each evaluation are archived alongside.  Raw
+public strain and pulse arrays are not duplicated: source locations and
+checksums are retained so the inputs can be retrieved from their public
+releases and the reported results audited.  \sup{S17} lists the archive
+identifiers, run commits and manifest digests.
